# ----------
# src/test/regress/parallel_schedule
#
# pg_regress starts each test as soon as its constraints allow, running at
# most --max-concurrent-tests tests (20 by default) and keeping their
# connections within --max-connections (40 by default, less if the server
# allows fewer) and their parallel workers within --max-parallel-workers
# (what the server can start, by default) at any time.
#
#   run: a b c [after: x y] [before: *] [notwith: p q]
#        [shared: r1 r2] [exclusive: r3] [workers: n]
#       Tests on one line are started without any constraint among
#       themselves.  "after:" names tests that must have finished before
#       these start, "notwith:" tests that must not run at the same time,
#       and "before: *" makes every later line wait for these.  Names must
#       be declared on an earlier line; "after: *" means every test on an
#       earlier line and "notwith: *" every test on another line.  "shared:"
#       and "exclusive:" name resources (arbitrary words): a test holding a
#       resource exclusively does not overlap any other test naming it,
#       shared users may overlap each other.  "workers:" gives the parallel
#       workers a test may have running at once, counting every statement
#       of the test and, for an isolation spec, every session of it; with
#       debug_parallel_query on, every test counts as one at least.  A line
#       without directives is kept apart only from lines that say so
#       ("notwith: *", "before: *").
#   test: a b c
#       A parallel group as in older schedules: waits for everything above
#       and everything below waits for it.
#
# Runnable tests start longest-dependency-chain first; the order of the
# file breaks ties.  Tests marked "notwith: *" can only start when nothing
# else is running, so they tend to run at the end.  A few tests reconnect
# (\c), so a handful of connections more than the budget can briefly be in
# use.
#
# The constraints were derived from recording which objects each test
# creates, drops, alters, reads and writes (including the targets of
# statements that fail on purpose), plus a few global effects (publications for all tables,
# database-wide VACUUM/ANALYZE/REPACK, event triggers, prepared transactions,
# large object OIDs, privileges on languages).
# The comments on the lines below name the recorded objects behind each
# constraint.  The worker counts are the most workers a test was seen to
# have running at once; the settings a test makes are only a lower bound on
# that, as a plan can have two Gather nodes active at once, a spec's
# sessions run their queries at the same time, and a VACUUM or an index
# build can use workers with nothing set at all.
#
# The catalog sanity checks run early with nothing else going
# on, before the tests that leave rule-violating objects behind;
# oidjoins runs late so that it sees as many objects as possible;
# event_trigger_login runs alone because its login trigger would catch
# connections of concurrent tests; sanity_check runs after
# everything else and alone, because its database-wide VACUUM moves rows,
# which changes the order an unordered SELECT returns them in, and
# filenode_mapping runs with it,
# checking the mapping of every relation after all the other tests rewrote
# theirs; tablespace runs last because it drops the tablespace created by
# test_setup.
#
# The file was generated from those recordings by a tool that is not part
# of the tree; to add a test, add a line with the constraints its objects
# need (a line without directives is fine for a self-contained test).
#
# Constraints marked "listing" come from catalog queries and psql \d
# commands that select objects by name pattern rather than by name: a test
# creating an object the pattern matches, in a schema the query looks at
# (other sessions' temp schemas included when it scans pg_class directly),
# must not run at the same time, and if it leaves the object behind, the
# listing test runs first.  Two more rules read the SQL: a test resetting
# the counters of a shared catalog (stats) is kept apart from tests writing
# that catalog, and the filenode_mapping check is kept apart from tests
# moving relations between tablespaces.
# ----------

